1.1 Plan for this lecture
The feedforward networks chapter trained its MLP with full-batch gradient descent, and lds’s chapter on gradient descent covers the algorithms that do better. This lecture runs them in PyTorch on the digits:

training on small random subsets of the training images instead of all of them at each step;

momentum and Adam as PyTorch optimizers;
changing the learning rate during training, starting with a short ramp up;
choosing the scale of the starting weights.

3.3 Three optimizers on the digits






ExrExercise 4 (Plain steps, momentum and Adam)






Exercise 4 (Plain steps, momentum and Adam) Suppose three fresh MLPs are trained for 5 epochs on minibatches of 32, using train() from Solution 1:

one with torch.optim.SGD at learning rate 0.1;
one with torch.optim.SGD at learning rate 0.01 and momentum 0.9;
one with torch.optim.Adam, PyTorch’s Adam, at learning rate 0.01.

Which reaches the lowest training loss?










SolutionSolution 4






Solution 4. 

Show codeoptimizers = {
    "SGD": lambda p: torch.optim.SGD(p, lr=0.1),
    "momentum": lambda p: torch.optim.SGD(p, lr=0.01, momentum=0.9),
    "Adam": lambda p: torch.optim.Adam(p, lr=0.01),
}
final_loss, test_acc = {}, {}
for name, make_optimizer in optimizers.items():
    mlp = make_mlp()
    final_loss[name] = train(mlp, make_optimizer(mlp.parameters()), 5, 32)[-1]
    with torch.no_grad():
        test_acc[name] = (mlp(x_test).argmax(dim=1) == y_test).float().mean().item()
print(final_loss)

{'SGD': 0.5297544598579407, 'momentum': 0.5874408483505249, 'Adam': 0.10584728419780731}

Show codeprint(test_acc)

{'SGD': 0.8860971331596375, 'momentum': 0.8844221234321594, 'Adam': 0.94807368516922}


Adam reaches the lowest training loss, 0.11, against 0.53 for plain SGD and 0.59 with momentum. It also classifies the most images of the test set correctly, 94.8%. Momentum and plain SGD end close together, as Solution 3 predicts: learning rate 0.01 with \(\beta = 0.9\) moves like learning rate 0.1 on a steady slope. A comparison like this depends on the learning rates chosen for each method, so a fair one tunes each method’s learning rate separately.

1.1 Plan for this lecture
The PyTorch chapter fit the soft 0-or-1 rule, a model with one parameter. This lecture builds models that learn their own features of the digits:

what a stack of linear layers can and cannot compute, and what a function applied between the layers changes;
how a network turns its last layer’s numbers into class probabilities and a loss, and how PyTorch computes that loss without overflowing;
how much a network with one layer of learned features can represent.

1.1 Plan for this lecture
Deep learning reuses the pieces of supervised learning from DATA 371 and DATA 471, which lds covers in full. This lecture reviews three of them on the handwritten digits from the course overview, then shows where each one reappears in deep learning:

the parts of a learning problem: a set of candidate rules, the parameters that pick one out, and a loss function;
why models are trained on a smooth loss rather than on their error rate;
why a model is judged on data it was not fit to.

1.1 Plan for this lecture
The syllabus covers how the course runs. This lecture is about what the course is for:

why hand-designed features run out, shown on a dataset of handwritten digits;
what representation learning and deep learning are;
where deep learning sits within machine learning and artificial intelligence;
a short history of neural networks;
the topics and tools of these notes.

1.1 Plan for this lecture
All code in these notes, and in the homework, uses PyTorch [1]. This lecture covers the three parts of PyTorch that everything else is built on, on the handwritten digits from the course overview:

how PyTorch stores the arrays that hold a network’s inputs, parameters and outputs;
how PyTorch computes the derivative of a loss from the code that computes the loss;
how PyTorch packages a model and the loop that fits it.

1.1 Plan for this lecture
Automatic differentiation gave the derivative of a loss without anyone writing it down. This lecture opens it up for the MLP on the digits:

how PyTorch records the operations that compute a loss;
how the chain rule, applied to those operations from the loss back to the input, gives the gradient with respect to every parameter at once, and how to compute it by hand and match PyTorch;
why that costs only a few times as much as computing the loss once;
why the gradient can shrink layer by layer in a deep network.

lds’s chapter on the same algorithm derives it for a general network with one hidden layer, and runs more experiments on depth.

5.1 Gradient sizes layer by layer






ExrExercise 9 (Gradients in a deep network)






Exercise 9 (Gradients in a deep network) Take two MLPs for the digits with 10 hidden layers of 32 units each, one with the logistic function as its activation function and one with the ReLU. At their starting weights, how does the size of the gradient of the mean training loss with respect to each layer’s weight matrix change from the last layer to the first?










SolutionSolution 9






Solution 9. 

Show codedef deep_mlp(activation, n_hidden=10, width=32):
    _ = torch.manual_seed(0)
    layers, n_in = [], 64
    for layer in range(n_hidden):
        layers += [nn.Linear(n_in, width), activation()]
        n_in = width
    layers.append(nn.Linear(width, 10))
    return nn.Sequential(*layers)


grad_norms = {}
for name, activation in [("logistic", nn.Sigmoid), ("ReLU", nn.ReLU)]:
    net = deep_mlp(activation)
    loss_fn(net(x_train), y_train).backward()
    grad_norms[name] = [m.weight.grad.norm().item() for m in net if isinstance(m, nn.Linear)]

fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(6, 3.5), layout="constrained")
for name, norms in grad_norms.items():
    ax.semilogy(range(1, len(norms) + 1), norms, marker="o", label=name);
ax.set_xlabel("layer (1 = first)");
ax.set_ylabel("size of weight gradient");
ax.legend();
plt.show()






Show codeshrink = {name: norms[-1] / norms[0] for name, norms in grad_norms.items()}
print(shrink)

{'logistic': 129510900.18186893, 'ReLU': 251.30350519888285}


The size of a matrix here is the square root of the sum of its squared entries. In both networks the weight gradient is largest at the last layer. With the logistic function, the weight gradient shrinks at every layer going back, and the last layer’s weight gradient is about \(10^{8}\) times the first layer’s. With the ReLU, the weight gradient shrinks over the last few layers and then levels off, and the last layer’s is 251 times the first layer’s. A training step with one learning rate would move the logistic network’s first layers almost not at all.

1.1 Plan for this lecture
The optimization chapter made training faster. This lecture is about what training finds: a network with many parameters can fit its training set far better than new images, and regularization trades some of that fit for better predictions. It covers

watching a validation set during training, and stopping at the best point;
weight decay in PyTorch’s optimizers;
randomly switching off units during training;
training on transformed copies of the training images.

2.1 Watching the validation loss






ExrExercise 1 (Watching the validation loss)






Exercise 1 (Watching the validation loss) How do the training loss and the validation loss of make_net() change over 300 epochs of fit()?










SolutionSolution 1






Solution 1. 

Show codeplain_net = make_net()
plain_history, plain_best_state = fit(plain_net)
best_row = plain_history.loc[plain_history["val"].idxmin()]
best_epoch = int(best_row["epoch"])
best_val_loss = best_row["val"]
final_row = plain_history.iloc[-1]
fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(6, 3.5), layout="constrained")
ax.plot(plain_history["epoch"], plain_history["train"], label="training");
ax.plot(plain_history["epoch"], plain_history["val"], label="validation");
ax.axvline(best_epoch, color="gray", linestyle=":");
ax.set_yscale("log");
ax.set_xlabel("epoch");
ax.set_ylabel("cross-entropy loss");
ax.legend();
plt.show()






Show codeprint(best_epoch, best_val_loss, final_row["train"], final_row["val"])

28 0.27951204776763916 0.0001481997751398012 0.3629544973373413


The training loss falls the whole time, to 1.5e-04 after 300 epochs. The validation loss falls to its lowest value, 0.280, at epoch 28, marked by the dotted line, and then rises to 0.363. After epoch 28 the network goes on fitting the training images in ways that do not carry over to new images: it overfits.

5.2 Definition






DefDefinition 3 (Data augmentation)






Definition 3 (Data augmentation) Data augmentation trains on transformed copies of the training inputs, made by transformations that should not change the target [1, Sec. 7.4]. Usually a new random transformation is drawn for each input at each epoch.










ExmExample 3 (Noise and shifts for the digits)






Example 3 (Noise and shifts for the digits)  

Show codedef add_noise(images, generator, sd=0.2):
    # Add independent normal noise with standard deviation `sd` to every pixel.
    return images + sd * torch.randn(images.shape, generator=generator)


def shift_images(images, rows, cols):
    # Move each 8-by-8 image by (rows, cols) pixels, filling the gap with 0.
    grid = images.reshape(-1, 8, 8)
    moved = torch.roll(grid, shifts=(rows, cols), dims=(1, 2))
    if rows &gt; 0:
        moved[:, :rows, :] = 0
    if rows &lt; 0:
        moved[:, rows:, :] = 0
    if cols &gt; 0:
        moved[:, :, :cols] = 0
    if cols &lt; 0:
        moved[:, :, cols:] = 0
    return moved.reshape(-1, 64)


example = x_train[:1]
noisy = add_noise(example, torch.Generator().manual_seed(0))
shifted = shift_images(example, 0, 1)
fig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 3, figsize=(6, 2.2), layout="constrained")
for ax, image, title in zip(axes, [example, noisy, shifted], ["original", "noise", "shifted"]):
    ax.imshow(image.reshape(8, 8), cmap="gray_r");
    ax.set_title(title);
    ax.axis("off");
plt.show()







The first training image is a 0. Adding normal noise with standard deviation 0.2 to every pixel leaves it recognizable, and so does moving it one pixel to the right. Both are candidate transformations for data augmentation on the digits.
